msg:
Letting a non-Lustre directory keep its record means the objects under it now reach printf_format_string() for the first time, and the %L directives are not quiet about them.

For a file, printf_format_lustre() takes the d == -1 branch:

    layout = llapi_layout_get_by_path(path, 0);
    if (layout == NULL) {
        llapi_error(LLAPI_MSG_ERROR, errno,
                    "error: cannot get file layout\n");

Off Lustre that ioctl answers ENOTTY, so `lfs find /mnt/lustre -printf '%p %Lc\n'` over a tmpfs-under-Lustre tree emits one error line per file on stderr; %Li, %Lo, %Lp and %LS go the same way, and %LF hits the same thing through llapi_path2fid(). The default message level is LLAPI_MSG_MAX, so none of it is suppressed.

Before this patch none of that was reachable -- cb_get_dirstripe()'s ENOTTY came out of cb_find_init() as an error and llapi_semantic_traverse() did `if (ret) goto err`, so the directory was neither printed nor descended.

The directive output itself is fine (*wrote stays 0), so it is only the noise. Should an object with no Lustre layout leave the directive empty without complaining, now that the walk reaches them?

For a CLF_RENAME record cr_namelen covers both names, not just the target one.

    /* mdd_dir.c, mdd_changelog_rec_ext_rename() */
    changelog_rec_name(rec)[rec->cr_namelen] = '\0';
    strscpy(changelog_rec_sname(rec), sname->ln_name, extsize);
    rec->cr_namelen += extsize;

The copy still reads back as the target name, since the embedded NUL is carried over, but it allocates and stores the source name after it. lustre_rsync.c bounds the same field with strnlen(changelog_rec_name(rec), rec->cr_namelen); worth doing the same here.

(style) This isn't a bug, but both arms do the same increment. Hoisting it reads as "always count, print when the caller asked for a FID":

    if (fc->fc_fid_when_lost && (fc->fc_rec->sr_valid & LLAPI_SCAN_FID))
            llapi_printf(LLAPI_MSG_NORMAL, DFID"%c", ...);
    if (fc->fc_unresolved != NULL)
            (*fc->fc_unresolved)++;

-type is neither in this refusal list nor always answerable, and the page does not say so.

scan_cl_mode() gives a type only for CL_CREATE, CL_MKDIR, CL_RMDIR and CL_SOFTLINK, so an object whose events in the window are all data stores coalesces without LLAPI_SCAN_TYPE. find_changelog_rec_cb() then counts it undecided and drops it:

    if (param->fp_type != 0 && checked_type == 0 &&
        !(rec->sr_valid & LLAPI_SCAN_MODE)) {
            st->fss_undecided++;
            return 0;
    }

So "lfs find $MOUNT --changelog all -type f" silently omits a file that was created before the window and written inside it, with only a count on stderr to say so. The --resolve entry below explains "undecided"; should this one mention that -type can land there without --resolve?

(defect) The open runs whatever sp_want asked for, but scan_rec_gather() returns at its first line when nothing in LLAPI_SCAN_MDT_MASK is set:

    if (!(want & LLAPI_SCAN_MDT_MASK))
        return 0;

LLAPI_SCAN_TYPE is not in that mask, so a caller passing sp_want = LLAPI_SCAN_TYPE - the name-only mode lustreapi.h advertises as performing no ioctl per object - pays a descriptor here that is never read, and inherits the ways it can fail. open_parent() wants O_RDONLY on the parent, so a FID resolving under a 0711 directory the caller does not own comes back -EACCES with no record delivered, even though sr_path, sr_name and sr_mode were complete before this point.

Could the whole block move under `if (want & LLAPI_SCAN_MDT_MASK)`?

(minor) llapi_scan_device() does not widen 0 the same way:

    dev.sd_want = sp != NULL && sp->sp_want != 0 ? sp->sp_want : ~0ULL;

and lustreapi.h says so in as many words - "0 means the scanner's own default, which is not the same for both". As written this reads as though editing the macro changes every entry point. Naming the two mount-side ones would match the code.

(suggestion) llapi_scan_rec_path() a hundred lines below does these same three steps - llapi_fid2path_at(), skip the leading slashes, compose `<mnt>/<rel>` with the root special case - and the commit message argues against exactly this kind of second copy. A record built here would let it be reused:

    struct llapi_scan_rec tmp = { .sr_fid = *fid,
                                  .sr_valid = LLAPI_SCAN_FID };

    rc = llapi_scan_rec_path(mnt_fd, mnt_path, &tmp, path, sizeof(path));

An overlong result then reports -ERANGE rather than -ENAMETOOLONG, which the man page would have to follow.
